# Thesis Source Map
## Relevant material in *On the Ultrametric Unconscious, Quantum Consciousness, and Mental Spaces* (2015)

This map records the thesis material that materially bears on unfolding, Mental Space, concepts, decomposition/gluing, cycles/boundaries, logical topology, presheaves, exact sequences, homotopy and homology. Page references are to the PDF page number, with the thesis's printed page usually one page lower in the main body.

The map is descriptive first; the audit judgment is listed separately so that the historical proposal is not silently repaired.

---

| PDF pages / section | Thesis item | Content recovered | Audit category |
|---|---|---|---|
| pp. 4–6, Introduction | Overall program | Mental Space with conscious/unconscious topologies; concepts as data; Matte-Blanco unfolding represented by distinguishing operator; logical topology, presheaves, exact sequences, simplicial complexes, homotopy and homology | thesis roadmap / heuristic |
| pp. 11–17, §0.2 | Topology, neighborhoods, continuity, covers, presheaf | Standard background intended to support distinguishability and concept relations | mostly standard, with some later misuse |
| pp. 17–22, §§0.3–0.5 | Ultrametric and p-adics | Ultrametric inequality, p-adic balls, claims about overlapping balls | standard core + overstrong ball claim |
| pp. 25–29, §0.8 | Simplex, simplicial complex, simplicial map, simplicial set, chain complex, boundary, cycles | Foundational algebraic-topology vocabulary used later for concepts and unfolding | standard ideas, several terminology/definition errors |
| p. 28 | Chain differential | \(\partial_k:C_k\to C_{k-1}\), alternating face sum, consecutive boundaries square to zero | standard genuine boundary |
| p. 29 | Cycles | kernel of boundary identified with cycles; thesis says zero-boundary chains will be special for concepts | standard definition + later heuristic |
| pp. 49–51, §2 | Mind / Mental Space | Mind consists of unconscious and conscious; Mental Space is a set of points endowed with topology; points are later treated as concepts | thesis-specific modeling definition |
| pp. 51–53, §2.1 | Concept | A concept is defined as a connected simplicial complex with every pair of points linked by an edge and no multiple edge | thesis-specific; repairable |
