# Thesis Source Map
## Relevant material in *On the Ultrametric Unconscious, Quantum Consciousness, and Mental Spaces* (2015)

This map records the thesis material that materially bears on unfolding, Mental Space, concepts, decomposition/gluing, cycles/boundaries, logical topology, presheaves, exact sequences, homotopy and homology. Page references are to the PDF page number, with the thesis's printed page usually one page lower in the main body.

The map is descriptive first; the audit judgment is listed separately so that the historical proposal is not silently repaired.

---

| PDF pages / section | Thesis item | Content recovered | Audit category |
|---|---|---|---|
| pp. 4–6, Introduction | Overall program | Mental Space with conscious/unconscious topologies; concepts as data; Matte-Blanco unfolding represented by distinguishing operator; logical topology, presheaves, exact sequences, simplicial complexes, homotopy and homology | thesis roadmap / heuristic |
| pp. 11–17, §0.2 | Topology, neighborhoods, continuity, covers, presheaf | Standard background intended to support distinguishability and concept relations | mostly standard, with some later misuse |
| pp. 17–22, §§0.3–0.5 | Ultrametric and p-adics | Ultrametric inequality, p-adic balls, claims about overlapping balls | standard core + overstrong ball claim |
| pp. 25–29, §0.8 | Simplex, simplicial complex, simplicial map, simplicial set, chain complex, boundary, cycles | Foundational algebraic-topology vocabulary used later for concepts and unfolding | standard ideas, several terminology/definition errors |
| p. 28 | Chain differential | \(\partial_k:C_k\to C_{k-1}\), alternating face sum, consecutive boundaries square to zero | standard genuine boundary |
| p. 29 | Cycles | kernel of boundary identified with cycles; thesis says zero-boundary chains will be special for concepts | standard definition + later heuristic |
| pp. 49–51, §2 | Mind / Mental Space | Mind consists of unconscious and conscious; Mental Space is a set of points endowed with topology; points are later treated as concepts | thesis-specific modeling definition |
| pp. 51–53, §2.1 | Concept | A concept is defined as a connected simplicial complex with every pair of points linked by an edge and no multiple edge | thesis-specific; repairable |
| p. 52 | Category of Concepts `Con` | Concepts as objects, intended inclusion morphisms | repairable category/poset idea |
| p. 53 | Related concepts | Two concepts are "related" if they have a point in common | thesis-specific |
| p. 53 | Cycles and symmetry conjecture | If concepts are mutually related there is a cycle; thesis suggests cycles / zero boundaries may indicate symmetry and nonzero boundaries asymmetry | heuristic; false as general theorem |
| pp. 53–55 | Concepts of order \(k\) | Concepts/categories represented by fixing first \(k\) digits/attributes in an \(m\)-adic representation | adapted from Khrennikov; mathematically usable hierarchy |
| pp. 55–58, §2.2 | Matte-Blanco unfolding | Quotations/paraphrases: conscious thought successively introduces asymmetrical relations; words differentiate; symmetrical being "unfolds" under asymmetrical analysis | psychological source material / heuristic |
| p. 58 | Kitten / cat | `kitten` treated as `young cat`; kitten is a proper subclass/refinement of cat; implication is one-way | strong refinement example |
| pp. 58–59, §2.3 | Definition of unfolding/distinguishing operator | \(\partial[v_0,\ldots,v_n]=\bigvee_j\neg v_j\wedge[v_0,\ldots,\widehat v_j,\ldots,v_n]\); omitted vertex is negated; \(\partial_v(\neg v)=0\); repeated application denoted \(\partial^n\) | central thesis-specific construction; under-typed |
| p. 59, Example 2.5 | Triangle unfolding | \(\partial(XYZ)=\neg XYZ\vee X\neg YZ\vee XY\neg Z\) (with one negated coordinate per term) | sound one-step Boolean operation on fixed factors |
| p. 60, Figure 3 | Logical triangle decomposition | Diagram expands the one-step decomposition into constituent logical regions/triangles | heuristic visualization |
| p. 60 | Tetrahedron repeated unfolding claim | Thesis says repeated application to a 3-simplex eventually gives the single-point minterms | points toward exact Hamming-layer model |
| p. 60, Example 2.6 | Printed second iteration | \(X\wedge\neg(Y\wedge Z)\vee Y\wedge\neg(X\wedge Z)\vee Z\wedge\neg(X\wedge Y)\) | inconsistent with exact rank-by-rank iteration |
| pp. 60–61 | Green box | `green ∧ box` is treated as an undifferentiated concept; unfolding gives `green XOR box`, interpreted as allowing the components to be independent/distinguishable | psychologically suggestive; XOR interpretation is too strong |
| p. 61 | Unfolding a tautological/symmetric expression | Thesis applies \(\partial\) to a more general disjunctive logical expression not covered by the original simplex-only definition | evidence of domain ambiguity |
| pp. 61–63, §2.3.1 | Gluing | Aim: reverse decomposition/integrate lower-dimensional pieces into higher concepts | central salvageable intuition |
| p. 62, Example 2.7 | Triangle gluing by conjunction | Printed Boolean formula is claimed to equal \(XYZ\) | false by truth table |
| pp. 62–63, Example 2.8 | Tetrahedron from triangles | Four 2-simplex faces are pictured as pieces of a 3-simplex; thesis speculates about an "integrator" and compares to CW complexes | heuristic; missing filler/provenance condition |
| pp. 63–67, §2.4 | Unconscious | Symmetry/indistinguishability, ultrametric topology, part-whole symmetry | psychological + mathematical modeling |
| p. 64 | Proposition 2.9 | Claims applying ultrametric principle to a topology yields the indiscrete topology | false |
| pp. 67–71, §2.5 | Consciousness and symmetry breaking | Consciousness associated with distinguishability/asymmetry and logical-wave measurement/collapse | speculative psychological/quantum analogy |
| p. 71 | Relation between collapse and unfolding | Thesis states \(\partial(X\,\text{tautological relation}\,Y)=X\mathsf{XOR}Y\) in the logical-wave discussion | domain extension not formally defined |
| pp. 71–73, §2.5.2 | \(\partial\) on exact sequences/logical expressions | Speculates about image/kernel, projection, exactness and distinguishing | mathematically defective as homological algebra |
| pp. 74–80, §3.1 | Logical Topology | Logical expressions/sets, open/closed sentence notions, ProLT, partitioned truth-set representations | partially formal but not a completed topology theory |
| p. 74 | Logical category/functor plan | Logical expressions mapped to sets; intended presheaves and sheaves; local sections to be glued | promising categorical aspiration |
| pp. 75–78 | LogEx, LSet, logical sets, covers, open/closed expressions, ProLT | Definitions intended to translate propositional connectives into set/topological operations | many type/closure issues |
| pp. 79–80 | Logical presheaf | Assigns logical data to open subsets with restriction morphisms and functoriality conditions | recognizable presheaf template; needs genuine base category |
| pp. 80–82 | Concepts as logical/partition sets | Concepts represented by logical attributes and discrete topologies; intersections extract shared attributes | useful finite observation idea |
| pp. 81–82 | Gluing higher concepts | Explicit statement that compatible sections on presheaves should glue to construct higher-order concepts | central gluing idea |
| pp. 83–85, §3.2.2 | Exact Logical Sequence | Short exact-sequence-like constructions with logical expressions/correspondence matrices | not standard exactness; defective |
| pp. 85–86 | Homotopic resolutions | "Homotopic" defined essentially by commutativity of morphisms between resolutions | false as definition of chain homotopy |
| pp. 86–87 | Unfolding two concepts | \(C_1=WXY\), \(C_2=XYZ\); coordinatewise unfolding terms shown; then comparison after unfolding | important concrete observation/refinement example |
| p. 87 | Pink flower | Higher concept `pink ∧ flower`; possibility that one subconcept is accessed first then associated with the other; evolution from simple to complex concepts | strong motivation for ordered refinement/gluing |
