## Strongest structural result

On the underlying world set, all observation refinement is preorder thinning:

`R_(Phi+Theta,S) = R_(Phi,S) ∩ R_(Theta,S)`.

Pre-T0 point splitting and post-T0 order thinning are two quotient-level manifestations of this one operation.

## Strongest new synthesis

For the coarse realized-signature poset `P` and fiber profiles `B_p`, define the lexicographic fiber expansion

`F = sum_{p in P} B_p`.

Then every refinement factors canonically as

`Q -> F -> P`,

where `Q -> F` is same-vertex descent thinning and `F -> P` is fiber expansion/projection. After T0, all `B_p` are singletons, so `F ~= P` and only thinning remains.

## Conditioning interaction

For a support restriction `T subset S`, the refinement/conditioning prism has:

- an always-cartesian thinning face;
- a fiber-expansion face cartesian iff every surviving coarse state retains every refined signature over it.

If an outcome event is measurable after refinement, this cartesian condition is equivalent to the event already being measurable before refinement. Newly enabled measurements are therefore exactly the non-cartesian fine-measurable cases.

## Homological program

Use the standard cone triangle for `Q -> F -> P` to split total refinement defect into thinning and expansion defects. Then define a mixed total-cofiber invariant for refinement/conditioning squares.

## Design/protocol program

Define a generalized relative observation-design problem on preorders. Static T0 separation contains Test Cover; post-T0 exact suborder design contains the current ProLT observation-dimension problem. Adaptive protocols are decision trees whose nodes carry `(S,Phi)` and whose edges execute `M_(theta=b) R_theta`.

The important new feature is branch-local ProLT topology/homology, not adaptivity by itself.

