\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}
\newtheorem{corollary}{Corollary}
\title{Vtree Alignment and Higher-Order Four-Corner Defects in Exact Boolean Factorization}
\author{Research draft}
\date{29 September 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We isolate two structural limits in exact AND-factor extraction from vtree-structured Boolean representations. First, maximizing the number of factors exposed by exact vtree cuts is a complete objective: its global optimum equals the semantic prime-factor count, while hard carrier-subtree constraints are compatible with semantic prime alignment exactly when the carrier and semantic block families are laminar. Second, a nonzero four-corner defect with varying supports $U$ and $W$ proves that some semantic prime block meets both supports, but this cannot in general be reduced to a pairwise interaction. The family supported only on $0^n$ and $1^n$ is semantically prime although every fixed-context two-variable slice has rank at most one.
\end{abstract}

\section{Setup}
Let $\Pi_f$ denote the canonical finest partition of the variable set for which a nonfalse Boolean function factors as a conjunction of functions on disjoint blocks. For a vtree $T$, let $E_T(f)$ be the finest partition exposed recursively by the exact compressed-SDD cut criterion.

\begin{theorem}[Exposure optimum]
For every nonfalse Boolean function $f$,
\[
\max_T |E_T(f)|=|\Pi_f|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every exposed factorization is valid, hence is a coarsening of $\Pi_f$. Conversely, build a binary vtree with one subtree for each block of $\Pi_f$ and combine those subtrees arbitrarily. Every internal split is a union of prime blocks against its complement and therefore factors; recursion reaches all prime blocks.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Carrier/semantic compatibility]
Let $\Pi$ and $\Sigma$ be partitions of the same ground set. A binary rooted leaf-labelled tree can contain every block of both partitions as a subtree leaf set if and only if the combined block family $\Pi\cup\Sigma$ is laminar.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Subtree leaf sets form a laminar family, giving necessity. Conversely, represent the inclusion poset of a finite laminar family as a rooted tree and refine nodes of degree greater than two by inserting binary nodes.
\end{proof}

\section{Defect support}
Consider a cut $A\mid B$ and a nonzero four-corner determinant witnessed by row assignments $a_0,a_1$ and column assignments $b_0,b_1$. Let $U\subseteq A$ and $W\subseteq B$ be the coordinates on which the two row and two column assignments differ.

\begin{lemma}[Support crossing]
Some block of $\Pi_f$ intersects both $U$ and $W$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If not, after fixing all nonvarying coordinates every prime factor depends on row variables only, column variables only, or neither. Its $2\times2$ corner table is rank one. Entrywise multiplication of rank-one outer products remains rank one, contradicting the nonzero determinant.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $U=\{x\}$ and $W=\{y\}$, then $x$ and $y$ are in the same semantic prime block.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No universal pairwise localization]
For $n\ge 3$, let $f_n(x)=1$ exactly for $x=0^n$ and $x=1^n$. Then $\Pi_{f_n}$ consists of one block, but every two-variable restriction obtained by fixing all remaining coordinates has matrix rank at most one. For every nontrivial cut $A\mid B$, a four-corner defect exists only when the witness changes all coordinates of $A$ and all coordinates of $B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A nontrivial product decomposition would make the two-point satisfying relation a nontrivial Cartesian product, which would contain mixed combinations; hence the function is prime. A fixed-context two-variable slice contains at most one of $0^n,1^n$, so it has at most one nonzero entry. Across $A\mid B$, the only two nonzero matrix entries are the all-zero and all-one row/column pairs; a nonzero $2\times2$ minor must select both, forcing all cut-side coordinates to change.
\end{proof}

\section{Interpretation}
The results support hyperedge-valued defect evidence rather than automatic pairwise must-link edges. They also show that carrier blocks should not be hard-wired into vtrees when they cross emerging semantic factors.
\end{document}

# Phase 5 — Carrier- and CM-Defect-Aware Vtree Adaptation for Exact Factor Exposure

## Role

You are acting as an independent research team specializing in:

- Sentential Decision Diagrams (SDDs);
- structured d-DNNF;
- vtree design and optimization;
- knowledge compilation;
- Boolean function decomposition;
- graph/hypergraph partitioning;
- exact SAT-based decomposition;
- Correspondence Matrices and four-corner rank certificates;
- experimental algorithmics.

This project is not to demonstrate that "a smart vtree helps."

The aim is to determine whether the simplicial carrier and/or CM four-corner defect information can be used to construct or adapt a vtree that exposes substantially more of the true semantic prime AND-factorization directly.

A theorem is desirable but not mandatory.

A rigorous empirical result or a NO-GO result is acceptable.

---

# 1. Materials

Use the attached:

    STRUCTURED_FACTORIZATION_PHASE4_2026-09-29.zip

The relevant Phase 4 baseline includes:

- `docs/SDD_DIRECT_EXTRACTION.md`;
- `docs/HYBRID_DIRECT_RESIDUAL_ALGORITHM.md`;
- `docs/INCREMENTAL_SAT_COMPARATOR.md`;
- `docs/PRIORITY_AUDIT.md`;
- `docs/NEXT_RESEARCH_GATE.md`;
- SDD validation code/data;
- persistent four-corner SAT code/data.

You may also use the Phase 3 package if supplied, particularly for the fixed-cut SAT baseline.

Treat previous claims as hypotheses to verify, not axioms.

---

# 2. Baseline theorem

For a compressed SDD at vtree split X | Y,

    f(X,Y) = OR_i [ p_i(X) AND s_i(Y) ],

Phase 4 established the exact cut criterion:

    f(X,Y) = g(X) AND h(Y)

iff the compressed decision has at most one nonfalse sub.

Recursively applying this criterion yields the exact finest factorization whose blocks are vtree subtrees.

If every true semantic prime factor is a vtree subtree, this becomes the unrestricted full semantic prime partition.

Thus the key limitation is not correctness but VTree Alignment.

---

# 3. Baseline alignment problem

The fixed balanced four-variable vtree

    ((x0,x1),(x2,x3))

was tested against all nonfalse four-variable Boolean functions.

Among the decomposable functions, a large majority had unrestricted prime partitions strictly finer than the fixed-vtree-laminar partition.

Therefore:

    fixed arbitrary vtree
        !=
    reliable exposure of semantic prime factors.

The task is to determine how much of this gap can be closed automatically.

---

# 4. Central research questions

Investigate two increasingly strong goals.

## Goal A — direct-factor exposure

Construct/adapt a vtree T such that the compressed SDD exposes as much as possible of the true semantic prime factorization directly.

Measure:

- fraction of prime blocks exposed;
- fraction of attributes resolved without SAT;
- number and size of residual blocks;
- number of residual four-corner SAT queries.

## Goal B — prime alignment

Identify a meaningful semantic class C and a computable vtree construction A such that

    f in C
        =>
    every semantic prime factor of f is a subtree of A(f).

This is the theorem-level target.

If no meaningful class survives, document the boundary honestly.

---

# 5. Important upper-bound control

For any known semantic prime partition

    Pi_f = {B1,...,Bk},

one can construct a binary vtree in which each Bi is represented as a subtree.

Therefore an oracle given Pi_f can be made prime-aligned.

Use this as an UPPER-BOUND CONTROL.

The interesting problem is not existence of an aligned vtree.

The interesting problem is recovering useful alignment WITHOUT already knowing Pi_f.

---

# 6. Algorithms to compare

Implement and compare at least the following.

## A. Carrier-initialized vtree

Start from the simplicial carrier prime partition Pi_K.

Construct a vtree in which each carrier block is contiguous/a subtree.

Explore alternative arrangements of the carrier blocks:

- balanced;
- size weighted;
- interaction weighted.

Determine when the carrier helps and when it actively hides semantic factors.

## B. Semantic interaction graph/hypergraph vtree

Construct a variable interaction structure from the succinct semantic representation.

Possible edges/hyperedges:

- variables co-occurring in a CNF clause;
- variables sharing a circuit gate/module;
- ANF monomial co-occurrence where relevant;
- minimal known semantic interactions.

Use graph/hypergraph hierarchical clustering or separator methods to build the vtree.

Do not assume syntactic interaction equals semantic prime dependence.

## C. CM four-corner defect driven adaptation

Use four-corner witnesses from the exact SAT oracle.

A failed cut gives a nonzero rank-two defect.

Investigate how such witnesses can guide:

- vtree rotations;
- subtree merges;
- "must remain coupled" scores;
- local cut replacement;
- interaction-edge learning.

Be careful:

A failed cut proves that the whole cut is invalid; it does NOT automatically identify which particular variable pair caused the global failure.

Determine what additional minimization/querying is needed to turn a cut defect into a sound pairwise or module-level constraint.

## D. Carrier + semantic hybrid

Combine:

    carrier structure
    +
    syntactic semantic interactions
    +
    exact CM defect evidence.

Test multiple weightings, but preregister the benchmark protocol before examining held-out results.

## E. Local vtree rotations

Starting from A-D, optimize by standard/local vtree rotations.

Possible objectives:

- number of direct factors exposed;
- estimated residual SAT cost;
- SDD size;
- combination of compiled size and residual cost.

Avoid using the ground-truth prime partition in the objective except for oracle controls.

## F. Oracle prime-aligned vtree

Construct directly from the known exact Pi_f.

This is not an algorithmic competitor.

It is the upper-bound control answering:

"How much could vtree alignment help if it were perfect?"

---

# 7. Theorem attacks

Attempt to prove guarantees for identifiable semantic classes.

Candidates include, but are not limited to:

1. positive irredundant CNF;
2. formulas whose interaction-hypergraph components equal semantic prime factors;
3. bounded-treewidth factor graphs;
4. read-once formulas;
5. the support-disjoint CM/LM operator-tree class from Phase 4;
6. formulas composed from already certified independent modules;
7. hierarchical/block decomposable functions;
8. classes where all four-corner defects are localized to a known interaction graph.

For each positive theorem, ask whether it is genuinely stronger than:

"if you already know the factors, put them in vtree subtrees."

A theorem must provide a computable construction from weaker information.

---

# 8. Defect-localization subproblem

Treat this as a serious mathematical subproject.

Given a cut A | B with

    rank_F2 [f]_(A|B) > 1,

the four-corner SAT solver returns assignments witnessing a nonzero 2x2 minor.

Determine what can be inferred about the actual prime factor partition.

